                        "entry_id": "badec63e-5bc9-3f59-a5af-e3691653fecd",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A second game played to reverse the outcome of the first; the means employed after the first turn of affairs."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "archaic"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2fba292d-57dd-49e2-90c7-1e2764aa3b51",
                                "sense_id": "c6dfe22d-f761-327b-be37-98582aa9def2",
                                "text": "1597, Michael Drayton, Englands Heroicall Epistles, London: N. Ling, 1603, “Charles Brandon Duke of Suffolk, to Mary the French Queene,”\nTwere ouer-sight in that at which we ayme, / To put the hazard on an after-game;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b7da1762-affa-41fd-ac91-f5d0c34e65e5",
                                "sense_id": "c6dfe22d-f761-327b-be37-98582aa9def2",
                                "text": "so many thousand faithfull and valiant English men, who left us in this libertie, bought with thir lives; losing by a strange after game of folly, all the battels we have wonn,"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "aca1017a-bae1-4741-853b-97decced0145",
                                "sense_id": "c6dfe22d-f761-327b-be37-98582aa9def2",
                                "text": "Our first Design, my Friend, has proved abortive; / Still there remains an After-game to play:"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "5bbb854f-d0a8-3db5-9f90-94e7f79fa3ff",
